WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.860 --> 00:00:06.300
I have the pleasure to introduce
two of our very own students,

00:00:06.300 --> 00:00:12.630
undergrad Itoro Atakpa and
graduate student Sergio Hiram

00:00:12.630 --> 00:00:13.970
Cantu.

00:00:13.970 --> 00:00:16.940
They will guide us in a
reflection on the life

00:00:16.940 --> 00:00:19.550
and legacy of Dr. King.

00:00:19.550 --> 00:00:24.130
We will hear first from Itoro
and then followed by Sergio.

00:00:24.130 --> 00:00:26.090
(OFF MICROPHONE) Itoro.

00:00:26.090 --> 00:00:29.030
[APPLAUSE]

00:00:35.400 --> 00:00:38.190
Good morning, everyone.

00:00:38.190 --> 00:00:39.510
My name Itoro Atakpa.

00:00:39.510 --> 00:00:40.722
I'm a junior here.

00:00:40.722 --> 00:00:42.180
I'm studying
mechanical engineering

00:00:42.180 --> 00:00:44.290
with a concentration
on product development.

00:00:44.290 --> 00:00:46.980
And as [? LuLu ?] said, I'm
your undergraduate speaker

00:00:46.980 --> 00:00:48.110
for this program.

00:00:50.769 --> 00:00:52.810
Oh, also, I want to thank
you all for coming out,

00:00:52.810 --> 00:00:54.230
before I jump right
into the speech.

00:00:54.230 --> 00:00:55.563
So thank you all for being here.

00:00:55.563 --> 00:01:00.220
I'm really honored to
present to you all today.

00:01:00.220 --> 00:01:03.350
It's been three years since
I sat in my makeshift study

00:01:03.350 --> 00:01:06.620
and pressed Submit on the series
of documents that were supposed

00:01:06.620 --> 00:01:10.550
to somehow represent my
character, my ambitions,

00:01:10.550 --> 00:01:12.450
and my potential.

00:01:12.450 --> 00:01:15.910
I was 16, and I had
labored for hours, stamping

00:01:15.910 --> 00:01:18.200
permanently onto a page
intentions and goals

00:01:18.200 --> 00:01:20.900
that today, I have not met.

00:01:20.900 --> 00:01:23.910
Because my expectations for what
I would grow to consider to be

00:01:23.910 --> 00:01:27.020
my crowning achievements
at MIT could have been

00:01:27.020 --> 00:01:29.150
based in no sense of reality.

00:01:29.150 --> 00:01:35.840
Because I, in reality,
didn't know what MIT was.

00:01:35.840 --> 00:01:39.870
MIT is the mecca of innovation
and technology, yes.

00:01:39.870 --> 00:01:41.990
The absolute greatest
place in the world

00:01:41.990 --> 00:01:45.240
to receive an engineering
education, yes.

00:01:45.240 --> 00:01:47.060
A space to conduct
advanced research

00:01:47.060 --> 00:01:50.204
and meet genius professors
and students, yes.

00:01:50.204 --> 00:01:52.370
But when I take a moment
to consider what has really

00:01:52.370 --> 00:01:55.830
shaped my experience and been
monumental in my growth here,

00:01:55.830 --> 00:01:58.820
it hasn't been the revving
arms of robots, which--

00:01:58.820 --> 00:02:02.330
don't get me wrong-- I do
love, but the warm arms

00:02:02.330 --> 00:02:04.904
of the community I've built.

00:02:04.904 --> 00:02:06.320
Over these two and
1/2 years, I've

00:02:06.320 --> 00:02:09.669
been constantly switching out
and reorganizing the pieces

00:02:09.669 --> 00:02:12.259
that construct my image of MIT.

00:02:12.259 --> 00:02:14.050
And I want to show you
all that this is not

00:02:14.050 --> 00:02:16.910
just an institute,
but a place to grow

00:02:16.910 --> 00:02:19.470
in your morals and
your leadership.

00:02:19.470 --> 00:02:21.600
Wide-eyed and anxious
in the classic freshman

00:02:21.600 --> 00:02:25.357
way in the fall of
2013, I did, in a manner

00:02:25.357 --> 00:02:26.940
of speaking, something
that could stop

00:02:26.940 --> 00:02:29.660
almost anyone in their tracks.

00:02:29.660 --> 00:02:32.990
I met MIT students.

00:02:32.990 --> 00:02:35.685
Yes, indeed, the most profound
moments in my experience

00:02:35.685 --> 00:02:37.310
here have been the
conversations that I

00:02:37.310 --> 00:02:40.120
had, not with
professors or advisors,

00:02:40.120 --> 00:02:43.570
but with the fresh-faced
20-year-olds who I happened

00:02:43.570 --> 00:02:46.990
to run into in the dining hall
before the chalk-dusted 6:30

00:02:46.990 --> 00:02:48.144
study sessions.

00:02:48.144 --> 00:02:50.810
The young men and women who wore
their pajama pants because they

00:02:50.810 --> 00:02:52.600
were that comfortable
with me, sharing

00:02:52.600 --> 00:02:54.560
experiences that
completely floored

00:02:54.560 --> 00:02:58.480
me and left me grappling
for a way to take action.

00:02:58.480 --> 00:03:01.760
I have, although not been able
to fulfill every eager promise

00:03:01.760 --> 00:03:03.830
on my application,
had the opportunity

00:03:03.830 --> 00:03:06.280
to do something remarkable.

00:03:06.280 --> 00:03:07.890
I've been able to
listen to my peers

00:03:07.890 --> 00:03:11.200
tell me about their
deepest insecurities,

00:03:11.200 --> 00:03:13.900
their qualms with the Institute.

00:03:13.900 --> 00:03:15.740
I remember one day,
about a year and a half

00:03:15.740 --> 00:03:17.490
ago, wanting to tell
my close friends

00:03:17.490 --> 00:03:20.350
who had been called the "n" word
and told that they don't belong

00:03:20.350 --> 00:03:23.290
here, something to
the effect of, that

00:03:23.290 --> 00:03:26.340
couldn't have happened at MIT.

00:03:26.340 --> 00:03:28.050
But it did.

00:03:28.050 --> 00:03:31.570
And things just like it have
happened again and again.

00:03:31.570 --> 00:03:34.230
I've heard my peers speak
about feeling unwelcome

00:03:34.230 --> 00:03:36.640
on their own campus,
afraid to speak up

00:03:36.640 --> 00:03:38.600
in class for fear of
getting the answer wrong

00:03:38.600 --> 00:03:41.190
and thus perpetuating
stereotypes.

00:03:41.190 --> 00:03:42.970
About feeling
invalidated in regards

00:03:42.970 --> 00:03:45.720
to how race and gender have
etched persistent obstacles

00:03:45.720 --> 00:03:50.546
into their experiences, told
to let it go and move on.

00:03:50.546 --> 00:03:52.420
About having been told
throughout their lives

00:03:52.420 --> 00:03:55.990
that they aren't supposed to
be smart, and subsequently,

00:03:55.990 --> 00:04:01.280
not forming a relationship, or
fearing their own intellect.

00:04:01.280 --> 00:04:04.390
About being told they don't
look like they go to MIT,

00:04:04.390 --> 00:04:08.240
or realizing that MIT makes
them no different from the men

00:04:08.240 --> 00:04:11.510
and women who have been
victims of profiling and police

00:04:11.510 --> 00:04:13.050
violence.

00:04:13.050 --> 00:04:15.495
About feeling abandoned,
unfit, under-represented,

00:04:15.495 --> 00:04:17.660
and without key resources.

00:04:17.660 --> 00:04:20.720
So many times we've laughed
helplessly and held hands,

00:04:20.720 --> 00:04:23.030
asking each other rhetorically
how we can possibly

00:04:23.030 --> 00:04:25.600
achieve complete
academic focus when

00:04:25.600 --> 00:04:27.380
faced with so many
problems beyond just

00:04:27.380 --> 00:04:30.110
the ones on our PSATs.

00:04:30.110 --> 00:04:33.470
MIT is not just an institute,
because it hasn't just

00:04:33.470 --> 00:04:36.970
given me an education in
the traditional sense.

00:04:36.970 --> 00:04:39.810
MIT introduced me to the
people who have infected me

00:04:39.810 --> 00:04:42.630
with their passion for building
a stronger community and a more

00:04:42.630 --> 00:04:45.247
inclusive society, and inspired
me to acquire positions

00:04:45.247 --> 00:04:47.830
within the Black Students Union
and the Black Women's Alliance

00:04:47.830 --> 00:04:49.246
in order to work
towards bettering

00:04:49.246 --> 00:04:51.930
campus life, opportunity,
and our understanding

00:04:51.930 --> 00:04:53.745
of each other.

00:04:53.745 --> 00:04:55.870
And fortunately, because
I've held these positions,

00:04:55.870 --> 00:04:58.779
I can say in a personal,
connected sort of way,

00:04:58.779 --> 00:05:00.320
that in this past
year in particular,

00:05:00.320 --> 00:05:02.236
we have truly improved
our level of discussion

00:05:02.236 --> 00:05:04.255
as a community on race.

00:05:04.255 --> 00:05:05.630
Although we have
certainly spoken

00:05:05.630 --> 00:05:08.807
in some capacity about diversity
and equality in the past,

00:05:08.807 --> 00:05:10.390
we've been more
deliberate in engaging

00:05:10.390 --> 00:05:13.370
the variety of sub-communities
and power levels

00:05:13.370 --> 00:05:15.607
than at any other time
since I've been here.

00:05:15.607 --> 00:05:17.940
We as undergrads have been
able to compose a strong list

00:05:17.940 --> 00:05:19.320
of recommendations.

00:05:19.320 --> 00:05:22.000
We as faculty, staff,
administrators, and students

00:05:22.000 --> 00:05:24.330
have been able to
form a sisterhood

00:05:24.330 --> 00:05:26.839
and share our stories
through My Sister's Keeper.

00:05:26.839 --> 00:05:29.130
We as a community have been
able to maintain a dialogue

00:05:29.130 --> 00:05:32.680
through Ask a Black Person and
other organized discussions.

00:05:32.680 --> 00:05:34.830
That level of impact
makes me extremely

00:05:34.830 --> 00:05:37.560
proud of the MIT community.

00:05:37.560 --> 00:05:39.880
Especially my peers,
who have stepped up

00:05:39.880 --> 00:05:42.400
and had the courage to
spearhead many of these efforts

00:05:42.400 --> 00:05:45.690
and to get so many
other people on board.

00:05:45.690 --> 00:05:48.000
It is for this
reason that I can say

00:05:48.000 --> 00:05:50.430
to my peers in the MIT
community, one thing

00:05:50.430 --> 00:05:53.200
that I know without
a doubt about them.

00:05:53.200 --> 00:05:56.560
You are a contagion.

00:05:56.560 --> 00:05:58.500
You are everything
they say you are

00:05:58.500 --> 00:05:59.940
in the books and the movies.

00:05:59.940 --> 00:06:03.700
Your words have this power to
hold like glue on the mind that

00:06:03.700 --> 00:06:07.160
takes them in, and then
compel that mind share

00:06:07.160 --> 00:06:08.900
them and spread them.

00:06:08.900 --> 00:06:10.850
And this is a power
that, like all, comes

00:06:10.850 --> 00:06:13.240
with great responsibility.

00:06:13.240 --> 00:06:18.944
I challenge you to infect
others with your voice.

00:06:18.944 --> 00:06:20.360
The voices in your
head don't have

00:06:20.360 --> 00:06:22.990
to be a silent and
personal affliction.

00:06:22.990 --> 00:06:27.700
They should be an epidemic
on the grounds of our campus.

00:06:27.700 --> 00:06:30.170
Because you are
the revolutionary.

00:06:30.170 --> 00:06:31.960
You are the difference.

00:06:31.960 --> 00:06:34.340
And I know from experience
that once someone catches

00:06:34.340 --> 00:06:37.220
wind of your emotion and listens
to your thoughts and ideas,

00:06:37.220 --> 00:06:42.822
the virus of your voice
is chronic and incurable.

00:06:42.822 --> 00:06:44.280
The greatest recent
example of this

00:06:44.280 --> 00:06:46.300
is the aforementioned
list of recommendations,

00:06:46.300 --> 00:06:49.540
which has now received backing
from the Academic Council.

00:06:49.540 --> 00:06:50.960
The list was made
possible largely

00:06:50.960 --> 00:06:52.376
because we have
been able to speak

00:06:52.376 --> 00:06:54.210
amongst ourselves consistently.

00:06:54.210 --> 00:06:56.240
To the point where we
all, through our own

00:06:56.240 --> 00:06:58.350
or through others'
experiences, have

00:06:58.350 --> 00:07:02.400
this mutual knowing of what
our challenges are here at MIT.

00:07:02.400 --> 00:07:03.990
We all can say in
one united voice

00:07:03.990 --> 00:07:07.680
that we need representation
and mental health.

00:07:07.680 --> 00:07:10.380
And that we need faculty
that truly values

00:07:10.380 --> 00:07:12.060
the well-being of the students.

00:07:12.060 --> 00:07:15.230
And that we need diversification
of our departments.

00:07:15.230 --> 00:07:16.950
And that we must
teach our students

00:07:16.950 --> 00:07:19.580
at least some basic knowledge
about the institutions

00:07:19.580 --> 00:07:22.190
of racism, and how they
are harmful to the growth

00:07:22.190 --> 00:07:24.240
of our society.

00:07:24.240 --> 00:07:27.190
Thus, as an undergrad
who is, in some sense,

00:07:27.190 --> 00:07:30.800
at least representing
some of us here today,

00:07:30.800 --> 00:07:34.404
I want to invite you
all to our table.

00:07:34.404 --> 00:07:35.820
And what I mean
by that is, I want

00:07:35.820 --> 00:07:38.080
us to further this trend
of the kind of conversation

00:07:38.080 --> 00:07:40.560
that made the list of
recommendations possible.

00:07:40.560 --> 00:07:42.920
I want us as the
entire MIT community

00:07:42.920 --> 00:07:45.800
to be deliberate in taking
our discussion about diversity

00:07:45.800 --> 00:07:49.150
down from the emails to the
types of everyday conversations

00:07:49.150 --> 00:07:52.956
that we don't have to suit
up for or be careful about.

00:07:52.956 --> 00:07:54.330
The kind that my
peers and I have

00:07:54.330 --> 00:07:55.760
at the dinner
table in McCormick,

00:07:55.760 --> 00:07:57.590
or the tables in
[? House ?] Two Kitchen,

00:07:57.590 --> 00:08:01.315
or the big round table
in the BSC Lounge.

00:08:01.315 --> 00:08:03.690
Where we don't have to navigate
so carefully that we miss

00:08:03.690 --> 00:08:06.450
some key points or sentiments.

00:08:06.450 --> 00:08:09.285
Everyday conversation,
especially when

00:08:09.285 --> 00:08:15.230
it transcends the boundaries
of faculty, staff, student,

00:08:15.230 --> 00:08:18.300
administrator, has
the power to lend you

00:08:18.300 --> 00:08:21.900
an enormous amount of
perspective, an amount that's

00:08:21.900 --> 00:08:24.500
completely unparalleled
by anything

00:08:24.500 --> 00:08:27.950
with a header and a salutation.

00:08:27.950 --> 00:08:30.410
Here at MIT, we
lead in innovation.

00:08:30.410 --> 00:08:31.779
We lead in research.

00:08:31.779 --> 00:08:33.570
We lead in the financial
and career success

00:08:33.570 --> 00:08:35.030
of our graduates.

00:08:35.030 --> 00:08:38.710
And now it is time for
us to lead in action.

00:08:38.710 --> 00:08:40.770
To set an example for
our peer institutions

00:08:40.770 --> 00:08:43.450
on how to conduct community-wide
diversity and inclusion

00:08:43.450 --> 00:08:44.860
efforts.

00:08:44.860 --> 00:08:47.880
As MLK once said, "The
function of education

00:08:47.880 --> 00:08:51.260
is to teach one to think
intensively and critically.

00:08:51.260 --> 00:08:54.210
Intelligence plus
character-- that

00:08:54.210 --> 00:08:56.590
is the goal of true education."

00:08:56.590 --> 00:09:01.750
We cannot allow the Institute
to exist without making

00:09:01.750 --> 00:09:05.770
a deliberate effort to develop
this kind of character.

00:09:05.770 --> 00:09:07.890
To encourage the facilitation
of the conversations

00:09:07.890 --> 00:09:11.380
that we continually avoid
on account of discomfort.

00:09:11.380 --> 00:09:13.400
If we truly want
continued progress,

00:09:13.400 --> 00:09:18.170
we have to learn that we are
not at the mercy of discomfort.

00:09:18.170 --> 00:09:20.520
We are not at the
mercy of discomfort,

00:09:20.520 --> 00:09:23.190
and we must learn
to overcome it.

00:09:23.190 --> 00:09:26.190
We must learn how to look
directly into someone's eyes

00:09:26.190 --> 00:09:29.560
when we say or listen to
something like, people

00:09:29.560 --> 00:09:33.590
don't feel wanted here.

00:09:33.590 --> 00:09:36.432
We have to explain
and hold accountable.

00:09:36.432 --> 00:09:38.140
We have to learn how
to listen to others'

00:09:38.140 --> 00:09:40.170
misunderstanding
of our sentiments

00:09:40.170 --> 00:09:41.826
without shutting down.

00:09:41.826 --> 00:09:43.200
Because in my
case, for instance,

00:09:43.200 --> 00:09:45.100
growing up in a
Nigerian household,

00:09:45.100 --> 00:09:48.594
and going to a subpar
public Georgia high school,

00:09:48.594 --> 00:09:50.260
and having separated
parents, and having

00:09:50.260 --> 00:09:52.390
some relatives who
went to MIT before me,

00:09:52.390 --> 00:09:55.940
and some who never made
it out of the village--

00:09:55.940 --> 00:09:58.500
all of these things matter.

00:09:58.500 --> 00:10:00.810
But we have to be proactive
in helping each other

00:10:00.810 --> 00:10:04.060
to understand why they matter.

00:10:04.060 --> 00:10:07.560
This is the only way to identify
and address the resources that

00:10:07.560 --> 00:10:09.340
are missing here.

00:10:09.340 --> 00:10:12.690
I say to my peers, speak.

00:10:12.690 --> 00:10:15.160
Speak about what
can be done better.

00:10:15.160 --> 00:10:17.650
Speak about how community
life can be improved.

00:10:17.650 --> 00:10:19.600
Speak, and others
will follow suit,

00:10:19.600 --> 00:10:23.080
because you are a contagion.

00:10:23.080 --> 00:10:24.350
Infect with your voice.

00:10:24.350 --> 00:10:26.570
And not every idea will
be gold, but every idea

00:10:26.570 --> 00:10:29.250
has validity and power.

00:10:29.250 --> 00:10:31.560
We must all speak.

00:10:31.560 --> 00:10:35.690
Because we've still got a
long way to go here at MIT.

00:10:35.690 --> 00:10:36.470
Thank you.

00:10:36.470 --> 00:10:39.520
[APPLAUSE]

